{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1438"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge geography/cities, geography/cities","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/geography__cities.g2.json quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/zz-r2-geography__cities.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",418],["-","1914"],["+","1914,"],["=",1],["-","at"],["+","during"],["=",3],["-","outbreak"],["+","First"],["=",1],["-","of"],["+","World"],["=",1],["-","war"],["+","War,"],["=",1],["-","with"],["+","because"],["=",1],["-","Germany,"],["+","„Sankt-Peterburg“"],["=",1],["+","sounded too German, "],["=",338],["-","2001."],["+","2001 – the city on the Hooghly where the nun Mother Teresa had worked with the poor and dying for half a century, and which much of the world still knows by its old name."],["=",127],["+","Germany has a chapter of its own. In the GDR, places were renamed to honour the heroes of the new state: Chemnitz became Karl-Marx-Stadt in 1953, and on 1 May 1949 the Brandenburg village of Neuhardenberg – itself named in 1814 after the Prussian chancellor Karl August von Hardenberg – became Marxwalde. After reunification both returned to their old names, Chemnitz in 1990 and Neuhardenberg on 1 January 1991, the village council speaking of the “removal of an old injustice”.\n\n"],["=",70],["+","Kolkata | Calcutta |\n| "],["=",7],["+","\n| Neuhardenberg | Marxwalde (1949–1990) |"],["=",78]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",388],["-","1914"],["+","1914,"],["=",1],["-","bei"],["+","während"],["=",1],["-","Kriegsausbruch"],["+","des"],["=",1],["-","mit"],["+","Ersten"],["=",1],["-","Deutschland"],["+","Weltkriegs,"],["=",7],["+","weil „Sankt-Peterburg“ zu deutsch klang, "],["=",312],["-","Kolkata."],["+","Kolkata – die Stadt am Hugli, in der die Ordensschwester Mutter Teresa ein halbes Jahrhundert lang mit Armen und Sterbenden gearbeitet hatte und die in Deutschland bis heute meist Kalkutta heißt."],["=",115],["+","Auch Deutschland hat sein eigenes Kapitel. In der DDR wurden Orte zu Ehren der Helden des neuen Staates umbenannt: Chemnitz hieß ab 1953 Karl-Marx-Stadt, und am 1. Mai 1949 wurde die brandenburgische Gemeinde Neuhardenberg – selbst 1814 nach dem preußischen Staatskanzler Karl August von Hardenberg benannt – zu Marxwalde. Nach der Wiedervereinigung kehrten beide zu ihren alten Namen zurück, Chemnitz 1990 und Neuhardenberg am 1. Januar 1991; der Gemeinderat sprach von der „Beseitigung alten Unrechts“.\n\n"],["=",64],["+","Kolkata | Kalkutta |\n| "],["=",7],["+","\n| Neuhardenberg | Marxwalde (1949–1990) |"],["=",70]]}]},
